                "content": "**Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\qquad &&\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd},\\\\[2mm]\n&\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\qquad &&\\text{if }n\\text{ is even}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) in the above set there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem, and no other value of \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Upper bound.**  \n   Introduce the change of variables  \n\n   \\[\n   u=a+b,\\qquad v=b-a .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   The set of required points is  \n\n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n        =\\{(u,v)\\mid 2\\le u\\le n+1,\\;u\\equiv v\\pmod 2,\\;\n          |v|\\le u-2\\}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); in the\n   \\((u,v)\\)–coordinates this means that the line is **not** parallel to the\n   axes \\(u=\\text{const}\\), \\(v=\\text{const}\\) and not vertical\n   (\\(v\\)–constant).  \n   For a sunny line the projection on the \\(u\\)-axis is injective, therefore\n   it can meet at most one lattice point of \\(S_n\\) for each admissible value\n   of \\(u\\).  Since admissible \\(u\\) are the integers \\(2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\) of a\n   fixed parity, the number of points of \\(S_n\\) lying on a sunny line is at\n   most  \n\n   \\[\n   m:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   A non‑sunny line (horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)) can contain at\n   most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  If exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are\n   sunny, counting points gives  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\,m+(n-k)n\\ge |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Solving (4) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\n   \\begin{cases}\n   n      & (n\\text{ odd}),\\\\[2mm]\n   n-1    & (n\\text{ even}).\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n\n   *Even \\(n=2p\\) (so \\(k_{\\max}=n-1\\)).*  \n   Take the \\(2p-1=n-1\\) sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_c:\\;y=x+c\\qquad(c=-p+1,-p+2,\\dots ,p-1).\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   All have slope \\(1\\).  \n   For a point \\((a,b)\\in S_{2p}\\) let \\(d=b-a\\).  \n   If \\(|d|\\le p-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_d\\).  \n   The remaining points are those with \\(|d|=p\\); they are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{(a,a+p)\\mid 1\\le a\\le p\\}\\cup\\{(a+p,a)\\mid 1\\le a\\le p\\},\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   and all satisfy \\(a+b=2p+1\\).  Hence they lie on the single non‑sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   D:\\;x+y=2p+1 .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus we have \\(n-1\\) sunny lines and one non‑sunny line, i.e. a configuration\n   with \\(k=n-1\\).\n\n   *Odd \\(n=2p+1\\) (so \\(k_{\\max}=n\\)).*  \n   Begin with the \\(n-2=2p-1\\) sunny lines of slope \\(1\\)\n\n   \\[\n   L_c:\\;y=x+c\\qquad(c=-p+1,-p+2,\\dots ,p-1).\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   They cover every point of \\(S_{2p+1}\\) except the two extreme points  \n\n   \\[\n   E_1=(1,p+2),\\qquad E_2=(p+2,1).\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   Add the two further sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   M_1 &: \\text{ through }E_1\\text{ and }(p+1,p+1),\\qquad\n          \\text{slope }-\\frac1p,\\\\[1mm]\n   M_2 &: \\text{ through }E_2\\text{ and }(p+1,p+1),\\qquad\n          \\text{slope }-p .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   (For \\(p=1\\) i.e. \\(n=3\\) one can check directly that the three lines\n   \\(y=x,\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\;y=2x-1\\) are sunny and cover the three points\n   of \\(S_3\\).)  \n   The slopes \\(-1/p\\) and \\(-p\\) are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore\n   \\(M_1,M_2\\) are sunny and distinct from the lines in (9).  Altogether we\n   have \\( (n-2)+2=n\\) sunny lines covering all of \\(S_{2p+1}\\).\n\n3. **Realising every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Starting from the maximal configuration of step 2, replace sunny lines one\n   by one with slope \\(0\\) (horizontal) or \\(\\infty\\) (vertical).  \n   A horizontal line \\(y=b\\) contains exactly the points of \\(S_n\\) whose second\n   coordinate equals \\(b\\); those points were already covered by the sunny line\n   that is removed, so the covering property is preserved while the number of\n   sunny lines decreases by one.  Repeating this operation we obtain configurations\n   with any number of sunny lines \\(k\\) satisfying the bound (5).\n\n   Consequently the set of attainable values of \\(k\\) is exactly the one stated\n   in the verdict.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nIntroduce the linear change of variables  \n\n\\[\nu=a+b,\\qquad v=b-a .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nThe map \\((a,b)\\mapsto(u,v)\\) is a bijection between \\(\\mathbb Z^2\\) and\n\\(\\mathbb Z^2\\); the condition \\(a,b>0\\) becomes \\(u\\ge2\\) and\n\\(|v|\\le u-2\\), while the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes \\(u\\le n+1\\).\nMoreover \\(u\\equiv v\\pmod 2\\) (because \\(u+v=2b\\) is even).  Hence\n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(u,v)\\mid 2\\le u\\le n+1,\\;u\\equiv v\\pmod 2,\\;|v|\\le u-2\\}.\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nA line in the original \\((a,b)\\)-plane has equation \\(b=mx+q\\) with\n\\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) iff, after the transformation (13), it is given by\n\n\\[\nv=(m-1)u+(q-m\\,u_0) \\qquad\\text{with }m-1\\neq0,\\;m\\neq0,\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\ni.e. it is **not** parallel to the axes \\(u=\\text{const}\\) or\n\\(v=\\text{const}\\) and is not vertical (\\(v\\)-constant).  Consequently the\nprojection of such a line onto the \\(u\\)-axis is injective: for each admissible\ninteger value of \\(u\\) there is at most one lattice point of the line.\n\nThe admissible values of \\(u\\) are the integers \\(2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\) having the\nsame parity as the chosen line; therefore a sunny line can contain at most\n\n\\[\nm:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(S_n\\).\n\nA non‑sunny line is either horizontal (\\(m=0\\)), vertical (\\(m=\\infty\\)) or of\nslope \\(-1\\).  In each case the line meets the triangle \\(\\{a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) in a\nsegment of length at most \\(n\\); hence it contains at most \\(n\\) points of\n\\(S_n\\).\n\nAssume that among the \\(n\\) distinct lines exactly \\(k\\) are sunny.\nCounting the points of \\(S_n\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\nk\\,m+(n-k)n\\ge |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nInsert the value of \\(m\\) from (16) and solve for \\(k\\).\n\n*If \\(n=2p\\) is even*, then \\(m=p\\) and (17) becomes  \n\n\\[\nk\\,p+(n-k)n\\ge n\\frac{n+1}{2}\n\\Longrightarrow k\\le n-1 .\n\\tag{18}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(n=2p+1\\) is odd*, then \\(m=p+1\\) and (17) yields  \n\n\\[\nk\\,(p+1)+(n-k)n\\ge n\\frac{n+1}{2}\n\\Longrightarrow k\\le n .\n\\tag{19}\n\\]\n\nThus\n\n\\[\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\nn      & (n\\text{ odd}),\\\\[2mm]\nn-1    & (n\\text{ even}).\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{20}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Configurations attaining the bound\n\n#### 2.1  Even \\(n=2p\\) – \\(k=n-1\\)\n\nConsider the \\(2p-1=n-1\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;y=x+c\\qquad(c=-p+1,-p+2,\\dots ,p-1).\n\\tag{21}\n\\]\n\nAll have slope \\(1\\) and are therefore sunny.\nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in S_{2p}\\) put \\(d=b-a\\).\nIf \\(|d|\\le p-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_d\\).\n\nThe points of \\(S_{2p}\\) with \\(|d|=p\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,a+p)\\mid 1\\le a\\le p\\}\\cup\\{(a+p,a)\\mid 1\\le a\\le p\\},\n\\tag{22}\n\\]\n\nand each of them satisfies \\(a+b=2p+1\\).  Hence they all belong to the single\nnon‑sunny line  \n\n\\[\nD:\\;x+y=2p+1 .\n\\tag{23}\n\\]\n\nThus the family consisting of the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines (21) together with the\nnon‑sunny line (23) covers the whole set \\(S_{2p}\\).\n\n#### 2.2  Odd \\(n=2p+1\\) – \\(k=n\\)\n\nFirst take the \\(n-2=2p-1\\) sunny lines of slope \\(1\\)\n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;y=x+c\\qquad(c=-p+1,-p+2,\\dots ,p-1).\n\\tag{24}\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover every point of \\(S_{2p+1}\\) except the two extreme points  \n\n\\[\nE_1=(1,p+2),\\qquad E_2=(p+2,1).\n\\tag{25}\n\\]\n\nAdd the two further sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nM_1 &: \\text{the line through }E_1\\text{ and }(p+1,p+1),\n      \\quad\\text{slope }-\\frac1p,\\\\[1mm]\nM_2 &: \\text{the line through }E_2\\text{ and }(p+1,p+1),\n      \\quad\\text{slope }-p .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{26}\n\\]\n\n(When \\(p=1\\) i.e. \\(n=3\\) one may use the explicit three sunny lines\n\\(y=x,\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\;y=2x-1\\); they cover the three points of\n\\(S_3\\).)  \nThe slopes \\(-1/p\\) and \\(-p\\) are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore\n\\(M_1,M_2\\) are sunny and distinct from the lines in (24).  Altogether we have\n\\((n-2)+2=n\\) sunny lines, and every point of \\(S_{2p+1}\\) lies on at least one\nof them.\n\n#### 2.3  Obtaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k_{\\max}\\) be the maximal number of sunny lines given by (20)\n(\\(k_{\\max}=n\\) for odd \\(n\\), \\(k_{\\max}=n-1\\) for even \\(n\\)).\nThe configurations of Sections 2.1 and 2.2 realise \\(k_{\\max}\\).\n\nTo decrease the number of sunny lines, replace a sunny line by a horizontal line\n\\(y=b\\) (or a vertical line \\(x=a\\)).  The horizontal line \\(y=b\\) contains\nexactly the points of \\(S_n\\) whose second coordinate equals \\(b\\); those points\nwere already covered by the sunny line that is removed, so the covering\nproperty is preserved while the number of sunny lines drops by one.\nRepeating this operation we obtain configurations with any integer\n\\(k\\) satisfying the bound (20).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nThe upper bound (20) is sharp: for even \\(n\\) the value \\(k=n-1\\) is attainable\nand for odd \\(n\\) the value \\(k=n\\) is attainable; by the replacement procedure\nevery smaller non‑negative integer also occurs.  No larger value of \\(k\\) is\npossible because it would violate (20).\n\nHence the complete set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\qquad &&\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd},\\\\[2mm]\n&\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\qquad &&\\text{if }n\\text{ is even}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]"
