  \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Solving (3) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\n   \\begin{cases}\n   n      & (n\\text{ odd}),\\\\[2mm]\n   n-1    & (n\\text{ even}).\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n2. **The case \\(k=n\\) for odd \\(n\\ge5\\) is impossible.**  \n   For odd \\(n=2p+1\\) equality in (3) would require  \n\n   * every sunny line to contain exactly \\(p+1=\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\)\n     points, and  \n   * the \\(n\\) lines to be pairwise disjoint on \\(S_n\\).\n\n   By Lemma 1 a sunny line can have at most one point on each diagonal\n   \\(a+b=s\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\)).  Since there are only \\(n\\) diagonals, a family\n   of \\(n\\) disjoint sunny lines can contain at most \\(n\\cdot n\\) points, but\n   the required number of points is \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2>n\\cdot n\\) for \\(n\\ge5\\).\n   Hence the equality in (3) cannot be attained when \\(n\\ge5\\) is odd, and\n   consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-1\\qquad (n\\ge5\\text{ odd}).\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(n=3\\) a direct inspection shows that \\(k=3\\) is attainable\n   (see the construction below).\n\n3. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   *Even \\(n=2p\\).*  \n   Take the \\(2p-1=n-1\\) sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_c:\\;y=x+c\\qquad(c=-p+1,-p+2,\\dots ,p-1).\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   For a point \\((a,b)\\in S_{2p}\\) put \\(d=b-a\\).  \n   If \\(|d|\\le p-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_d\\).  \n   The remaining points are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{(a,a+p)\\mid 1\\le a\\le p\\}\\cup\\{(a+p,a)\\mid 1\\le a\\le p\\},\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   all of which satisfy \\(a+b=2p+1\\); they lie on the single non‑sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   D:\\;x+y=2p+1 .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus we have \\(n-1\\) sunny lines and one non‑sunny line, i.e. a configuration\n   with \\(k=n-1\\).\n\n   *Odd \\(n=2p+1\\ge5\\).*  \n   Use the same family (6) of \\(2p-1=n-2\\) sunny lines of slope 1.\n   They cover every point of \\(S_{2p+1}\\) except the two “extreme’’ points  \n\n   \\[\n   E_1=(1,p+2),\\qquad E_2=(p+2,1).\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Add the horizontal line  \n\n   \\[\n   H:\\;y=1\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   (non‑sunny) which contains the points \\((1,1),(2,1),\\dots ,(p+2,1)\\).\n   The point \\(E_2\\) is already on \\(H\\); the point \\(E_1\\) is covered by the\n   sunny line \\(L_{p+1}: y=x+(p+1)\\), which is **not** among the lines (6) because\n   its constant term exceeds \\(p-1\\).  Hence we obtain a family consisting of\n   \\(n-1\\) sunny lines (the \\(2p-1\\) lines (6) together with \\(L_{p+1}\\)) and\n   one non‑sunny line \\(H\\).  All points of \\(S_{2p+1}\\) are covered, so\n   \\(k=n-1\\) is attainable for every odd \\(n\\ge5\\).\n\n   *The case \\(n=3\\).*  \n   The three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=x,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\qquad y=2x-1\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   cover the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\); thus \\(k=3\\) is possible.\n\n4. **Realising every smaller \\(k\\).**  \n   Starting from a configuration with the maximal admissible number of sunny\n   lines (the constructions above), replace a sunny line by a horizontal (or\n   vertical) line.  The replacement does not destroy the covering property,\n   because the horizontal (vertical) line contains exactly the points of\n   \\(S_n\\) that had the same second (first) coordinate as the removed sunny\n   line.  Repeating this operation we obtain configurations with any integer\n   \\(k\\) satisfying the bound (4).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nIntroduce the linear change of variables  \n\n\\[\nu=a+b,\\qquad v=b-a .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nThe map \\((a,b)\\mapsto(u,v)\\) is a bijection of \\(\\mathbb Z^2\\).  \nThe condition \\(a,b>0\\) becomes \\(u\\ge2\\) and \\(|v|\\le u-2\\); the condition\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes \\(u\\le n+1\\).  Moreover \\(u\\equiv v\\pmod 2\\) because\n\\(u+v=2b\\) is even.  Hence  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(u,v)\\mid 2\\le u\\le n+1,\\;u\\equiv v\\pmod 2,\\;|v|\\le u-2\\}.\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nA sunny line is not parallel to the axes \\(u=\\text{const}\\) or\n\\(v=\\text{const}\\); therefore its projection on the \\(u\\)-axis is injective.\nConsequently for each admissible integer value of \\(u\\) the line meets at most\none lattice point of \\(S_n\\).  The admissible values of \\(u\\) are the integers\n\\(2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\) having a fixed parity; their number is  \n\n\\[\nm:=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nThus\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{A sunny line contains at most }m\\text{ points of }S_n.}\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nA non‑sunny line (horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)) meets the triangle\n\\(\\{a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) in a segment of length at most \\(n\\); therefore it contains\nat most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\nAssume that among the \\(n\\) distinct lines exactly \\(k\\) are sunny.\nCounting the points of \\(S_n\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nk\\,m+(n-k)n\\ge |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(m\\) from (15) and solve for \\(k\\).\n\n*If \\(n=2p\\) is even*, then \\(m=p\\) and (17) becomes  \n\n\\[\nk\\,p+(n-k)n\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\Longrightarrow k\\le n-1 .\n\\tag{18}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(n=2p+1\\) is odd*, then \\(m=p+1\\) and (17) yields  \n\n\\[\nk\\,(p+1)+(n-k)n\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\Longrightarrow k\\le n .\n\\tag{19}\n\\]\n\nHence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\nn      & (n\\text{ odd}),\\\\[2mm]\nn-1    & (n\\text{ even}).\n\\end{cases}}\n\\tag{20}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The value \\(k=n\\) is impossible for odd \\(n\\ge5\\)\n\nLet \\(n=2p+1\\ge5\\) be odd and suppose that a configuration with \\(k=n\\)\nsunny lines exists.  Equality must hold in (19); therefore each sunny line\ncontains exactly \\(p+1=m\\) points of \\(S_n\\), and the \\(n\\) lines are pairwise\ndisjoint on \\(S_n\\).\n\nA sunny line cannot contain two points with the same sum \\(a+b\\): if\n\\((a_1,b_1)\\) and \\((a_2,b_2)\\) satisfy \\(a_1+b_1=a_2+b_2\\) and lie on a\nline \\(b=ma+q\\) with \\(m\\neq-1\\), then  \n\n\\[\n(1+m)a_1+q=(1+m)a_2+q\\Longrightarrow a_1=a_2,\n\\]\n\nhence the points coincide.  Consequently a sunny line meets each diagonal\n\\(\\{a+b=s\\}\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\)) in **at most one** point.  There are exactly\n\\(n\\) diagonals, so a family of \\(n\\) disjoint sunny lines can contain at most\n\\(n\\cdot n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  But  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>n\\cdot n\\qquad (n\\ge5),\n\\]\n\na contradiction.  Therefore for odd \\(n\\ge5\\)\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-1 } .\n\\tag{21}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) a direct construction (see §3) shows that \\(k=3\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Constructions attaining the maximal admissible number of sunny lines\n\n#### 3.1  Even \\(n=2p\\) (maximal \\(k=n-1\\))\n\nTake the \\(2p-1=n-1\\) sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;y=x+c\\qquad(c=-p+1,-p+2,\\dots ,p-1).\n\\tag{22}\n\\]\n\nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in S_{2p}\\) put \\(d=b-a\\).  \nIf \\(|d|\\le p-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_d\\).  \nThe points with \\(|d|=p\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,a+p)\\mid1\\le a\\le p\\}\\cup\\{(a+p,a)\\mid1\\le a\\le p\\},\n\\tag{23}\n\\]\n\nall satisfying \\(a+b=2p+1\\); they lie on the non‑sunny line  \n\n\\[\nD:\\;x+y=2p+1 .\n\\tag{24}\n\\]\n\nThus we have \\(n-1\\) sunny lines and one non‑sunny line, i.e. a configuration\nwith \\(k=n-1\\).\n\n#### 3.2  Odd \\(n=2p+1\\ge5\\) (maximal \\(k=n-1\\))\n\nBegin with the \\(2p-1=n-2\\) sunny lines of slope 1 given by (22).  \nThey cover every point of \\(S_{2p+1}\\) except the two points  \n\n\\[\nE_1=(1,p+2),\\qquad E_2=(p+2,1).\n\\tag{25}\n\\]\n\nAdd the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_{p+1}:\\;y=x+(p+1)\n\\tag{26}\n\\]\n\nwhich contains \\(E_1\\) and, together with the previous family, covers all\npoints whose difference \\(b-a\\) lies in \\(\\{-p,\\dots ,p\\}\\).  \nFinally add the horizontal non‑sunny line  \n\n\\[\nH:\\;y=1 .\n\\tag{27}\n\\]\n\n\\(H\\) contains \\(E_2\\) and all points \\((a,1)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le p+2\\); the\nremaining points are already covered by the sunny lines.  Hence we obtain a\nfamily consisting of \\(n-1\\) sunny lines (the \\(2p-1\\) lines (22) together\nwith \\(L_{p+1}\\)) and one non‑sunny line \\(H\\).\n\n#### 3.3  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\ny=x,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\qquad y=2x-1\n\\tag{28}\n\\]\n\ncover the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\); thus \\(k=3\\) is possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Obtaining every smaller value of \\(k\\)\n\nStarting from a configuration that realises the maximal admissible number of\nsunny lines (the families described in §3), replace a sunny line by a\nhorizontal (or vertical) line.  The replacement does not destroy the covering\nproperty, because a horizontal line \\(y=b\\) contains exactly the points of\n\\(S_n\\) whose second coordinate equals \\(b\\); those points were already covered\nby the sunny line that is removed.  Repeating the replacement we can reduce\nthe number of sunny lines one by one, thereby obtaining configurations with\nany integer \\(k\\) satisfying the bound (20).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Final answer\n\nCombining the upper bound (20) with the constructions of §3 and the reduction\nprocedure of §4 we obtain the complete description of the admissible values\nof \\(k\\):\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\} &&\\text{if }n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n&\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\} &&\\text{if }n\\ge4 .\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\nThus for every \\(n\\ge3\\) all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) in the above set can\nbe realised, and no other value of \\(k\\) is possible. ∎\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
