h the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\} &&\\text{if }n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n&\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\} &&\\text{if }n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\nIn particular the maximal possible number of sunny lines equals  \n\n\\[\nk_{\\max }=\n\\begin{cases}\nn      & (n=3),\\\\[2mm]\nn-1    & (n\\ge 4).\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Upper bound.**  \n   – Transform the lattice points \\((a,b)\\) to \\((u,v)=(a+b,\\;b-a)\\).  \n   – A sunny line is not parallel to the axes in the \\((u,v)\\)–plane; consequently it meets each vertical line \\(u=\\text{const}\\) in at most one lattice point.  \n   – Hence a sunny line can contain at most \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) points of the set  \n\n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   – A non‑sunny line (horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   – Counting the points covered by the \\(n\\) lines gives  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac n2\\Bigr\\rceil+(n-k)n\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Solving (2) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\n   \\begin{cases}\n   n      & (n\\text{ odd}),\\\\[2mm]\n   n-1    & (n\\text{ even}).\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   For odd \\(n\\ge5\\) equality in (3) would force the \\(n\\) sunny lines to be pairwise disjoint on \\(S_n\\); this is impossible because a sunny line meets each diagonal \\(\\{a+b=s\\}\\) in at most one point, while \\(|S_n|>\\!n^2\\).  Hence for odd \\(n\\ge5\\) we also have \\(k\\le n-1\\).  For \\(n=3\\) the bound \\(k\\le3\\) is sharp.\n\n2. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   The construction is performed on the set  \n\n   \\[\n   S_{n-1}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n\\},\n   \\qquad |S_{n-1}|=\\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   *Even \\(n=2p\\).*  \n   Partition \\(S_{2p-1}\\) into \\(2p-1\\) disjoint sunny lines, each containing exactly \\(p\\) points.  \n   For \\(i=1,\\dots ,2p-1\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y=\\frac{-i}{p}\\,x+\\frac{i(p+1)}{p}.\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(L_i\\) meets the diagonals \\(a+b=2,3,\\dots ,2p\\) in the points  \n\n   \\[\n   \\bigl(1,\\;i\\bigr),\\;\n   \\bigl(2,\\;i-1\\bigr),\\dots ,\n   \\bigl(p,\\;i-p+1\\bigr),\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   all of which belong to \\(S_{2p-1}\\); there are exactly \\(p\\) of them.  \n   The slopes \\(-i/p\\) are never \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so every \\(L_i\\) is sunny, and the families \\(\\{L_i\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint on \\(S_{2p-1}\\).  \n   Adding the non‑sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   D:\\;x+y=2p+1\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   (which contains all points with \\(a+b=2p+1\\)) yields a configuration of \\(2p\\) lines, exactly \\(2p-1=n-1\\) of which are sunny and which covers the whole set \\(S_{2p}\\).\n\n   *Odd \\(n=2p+1\\ge5\\).*  \n   First use the same \\(2p-1\\) sunny lines \\(L_i\\) from (5) (they cover all points of \\(S_{2p}\\) except the two “corner’’ points \\((1,p+2)\\) and \\((p+2,1)\\)).  \n   Add the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{p+1}:\\; y=x+(p+1)\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   which contains \\((1,p+2)\\).  \n   Finally add the horizontal non‑sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   H:\\;y=1 ,\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   which contains \\((p+2,1)\\) and all points \\((a,1)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le p+2\\).  \n   The total number of lines is \\((2p-1)+1+1=2p+1=n\\); exactly \\(n-1\\) of them are sunny.\n\n   *The case \\(n=3\\).*  \n   The three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=x,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\qquad y=2x-1\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   cover the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\); hence \\(k=3\\) is attainable.\n\n3. **Realising every smaller \\(k\\).**  \n   Starting from a configuration that attains the maximal value \\(k_{\\max}\\) (the families described in §2), replace a sunny line by a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) (or a vertical line \\(x=c\\)).  \n   The horizontal line contains exactly the points of \\(S_n\\) whose second coordinate equals \\(c\\); those points were already covered by the sunny line that is removed, so the covering property is preserved while the number of sunny lines is reduced by one.  Repeating this operation we obtain configurations with any integer \\(k\\) satisfying the bound (3).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nIntroduce the linear change of variables  \n\n\\[\nu=a+b,\\qquad v=b-a .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe map \\((a,b)\\mapsto(u,v)\\) is a bijection of \\(\\mathbb Z^2\\).  \nIn the \\((u,v)\\)-plane the set \\(S_n\\) becomes  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(u,v)\\mid 2\\le u\\le n+1,\\;u\\equiv v\\pmod 2,\\;|v|\\le u-2\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nA line in the \\((a,b)\\)-plane with equation \\(\\alpha a+\\beta b=\\gamma\\) is transformed into  \n\n\\[\n(\\alpha+\\beta)u+(\\beta-\\alpha)v=2\\gamma .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nHence a line is **non‑sunny** precisely when one of the coefficients of\n\\(u\\) or \\(v\\) in (4) vanishes; a sunny line has both coefficients non‑zero,\ni.e. it is not parallel to either the \\(u\\)- or the \\(v\\)-axis.\n\nConsequently a sunny line meets each vertical line \\(u=\\text{const}\\) in at most\none lattice point of \\(S_n\\).  The admissible integer values of \\(u\\) are\n\\(2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\); among them exactly  \n\n\\[\nm:=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac n2\\Bigr\\rceil\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nhave the same parity as the corresponding \\(v\\).  Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{A sunny line contains at most }m\\text{ points of }S_n.}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nA non‑sunny line (horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)) intersects the\ntriangle \\(\\{a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) in a segment of length at most \\(n\\); thus it\ncontains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\nAssume that among the \\(n\\) distinct lines exactly \\(k\\) are sunny.  Counting\nthe points of \\(S_n\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\nk\\,m+(n-k)n\\ge |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(m\\) from (5) and solve for \\(k\\).\n\n*If \\(n=2p\\) is even*, then \\(m=p\\) and (7) gives  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(n=2p+1\\) is odd*, then \\(m=p+1\\) and (7) yields  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nFor odd \\(n\\ge5\\) equality in (9) would force the \\(n\\) sunny lines to be\npairwise disjoint on \\(S_n\\).  A sunny line can meet each diagonal\n\\(\\{a+b=s\\}\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\)) in at most one point; there are only \\(n\\)\nsuch diagonals, so at most \\(n\\cdot n\\) points could be covered.  Since  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>n^2\\qquad (n\\ge5),\n\\]\n\nthe equality cannot occur.  Hence for odd \\(n\\ge5\\) we also have  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the bound \\(k\\le3\\) is attainable (see §2.3).\n\nSummarising,\n\n\\[\nk_{\\max }=\n\\begin{cases}\nn      & (n=3),\\\\[2mm]\nn-1    & (n\\ge4).\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Constructions attaining the bound\n\nWe construct, for every \\(n\\ge4\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering\n\\(S_n\\) and containing exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\nDenote  \n\n\\[\nT_{n-1}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n\\},\n\\qquad |T_{n-1}|=\\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nIf we can partition \\(T_{n-1}\\) into \\(n-1\\) disjoint sunny lines, each\ncontaining exactly \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) points, then adding the single\nnon‑sunny line  \n\n\\[\nD:\\;x+y=n+1\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n(which contains all points with \\(a+b=n+1\\)) yields the required configuration.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  Even \\(n=2p\\)\n\nFor \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,2p-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=\\frac{-i}{p}\\,x+\\frac{i(p+1)}{p}.\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\n*Sunny.*  The slope \\(-i/p\\) is never \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\n\n*Points of \\(L_i\\) in \\(T_{2p-1}\\).*  \nWriting the points of \\(L_i\\) as \\((x,y)=(t,\\; -\\tfrac{i}{p}t+\\tfrac{i(p+1)}{p})\\) and imposing the\nintegrality conditions \\(x,y\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\) and \\(x+y\\le2p\\) one obtains exactly the\n\\(p\\) lattice points  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(1,i\\bigr),\\;\n\\bigl(2,i-1\\bigr),\\dots ,\n\\bigl(p,i-p+1\\bigr).\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nAll of them satisfy \\(1\\le x\\le p\\), \\(1\\le y\\le p\\) and \\(x+y\\le2p\\); hence they belong to\n\\(T_{2p-1}\\).  No other lattice point of \\(T_{2p-1}\\) lies on \\(L_i\\).\n\n*Disjointness.*  For distinct indices \\(i\\neq j\\) the sets (15) are disjoint,\nbecause the second coordinate in (15) equals the index \\(i\\) for the first point\nand decreases by one at each step; two different indices cannot produce the same\nordered pair.\n\nThus the family \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{2p-1}\\) partitions \\(T_{2p-1}\\) into \\(2p-1=n-1\\)\nsunny lines, each containing exactly \\(p=n/2\\) points.\n\nAdding the non‑sunny line \\(D:x+y=2p+1\\) gives a configuration of \\(n\\) lines\ncovering the whole set \\(S_{2p}\\) with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Odd \\(n=2p+1\\;(p\\ge2)\\)\n\nStart with the same \\(2p-1\\) sunny lines \\(L_i\\) defined in (14); they cover all\npoints of \\(T_{2p}\\) except the two “extreme’’ points  \n\n\\[\nE_1=(1,p+2),\\qquad E_2=(p+2,1).\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nAdd the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_{p+1}:\\; y=x+(p+1),\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nwhich contains \\(E_1\\) (and also the point \\((p+1,2p+1)\\) that lies outside\n\\(S_{2p+1}\\), so no conflict arises).  Finally add the horizontal non‑sunny line  \n\n\\[\nH:\\; y=1 .\n\\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(H\\) contains \\(E_2\\) and all points \\((a,1)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le p+2\\);\nthose points were not covered before, so the covering property is restored.\nThe total number of lines is \\((2p-1)+1+1=2p+1=n\\); exactly \\(n-1\\) of them\n(the \\(2p-1\\) lines \\(L_i\\) together with \\(L_{p+1}\\)) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\ny=x,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\qquad y=2x-1\n\\tag{19}\n\\]\n\ncover the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\); hence \\(k=3\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Obtaining every smaller value of \\(k\\)\n\nLet a configuration with the maximal admissible number of sunny lines be given\n(the families constructed in §2).  Choose any sunny line \\(L\\) of that\nconfiguration and replace it by a horizontal line \\(y=c\\), where \\(c\\) is the\nsecond coordinate of any point of \\(L\\).  The horizontal line contains exactly\nthe points of \\(S_n\\) whose second coordinate equals \\(c\\); those points were\nalready covered by \\(L\\).  Hence the covering property is preserved while the\nnumber of sunny lines decreases by one.  Repeating this operation we obtain\nconfigurations with any integer \\(k\\) satisfying the bound (11).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Final answer\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\} &&\\text{if }n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n&\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\} &&\\text{if }n\\ge4 .\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\nThe bound is sharp: the constructions above realise the maximal value\n\\(k_{\\max}=n\\) for \\(n=3\\) and \\(k_{\\max}=n-1\\) for all \\(n\\ge4\\); by the\nreplacement procedure every smaller non‑negative integer \\(k\\) is also\nrealisable. ∎\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
