 \\[\n     L_{p+1}:\\; y=x+(p+1)\n     \\tag{11}\n     \\]\n\n     which contains \\(C_1\\), and add the non‑sunny line  \n\n     \\[\n     H:\\; y=1 .\n     \\tag{12}\n     \\]\n\n     The line \\(H\\) contains \\(C_2\\) and all points \\((a,1)\\) with\n     \\(1\\le a\\le p+2\\); those points were not covered before.\n     The total number of lines is \\((2p-1)+1+1=n\\), and exactly\n     \\(n-1\\) of them (the \\(2p-1\\) lines \\(L_i\\) together with \\(L_{p+1}\\))\n     are sunny.\n\n   *The case \\(n=3\\).*  \n     The three sunny lines  \n\n     \\[\n     y=x,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\qquad y=2x-1\n     \\tag{13}\n     \\]\n\n     cover the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\); therefore \\(k=3\\) is\n     attainable.\n\n3. **Realising every smaller admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Start from a configuration that attains the maximal value\n   \\(k_{\\max}\\) described above.\n   Choose a sunny line \\(L\\) and replace it by a horizontal line\n   \\(y=c\\) where \\(c\\) is the second coordinate of any point of \\(L\\).\n   The horizontal line contains exactly the points of \\(S_n\\) whose\n   second coordinate equals \\(c\\); those points were already covered by\n   \\(L\\).  Hence the covering property is preserved while the number of\n   sunny lines decreases by one.  Repeating this operation we obtain\n   configurations with any integer \\(k\\) satisfying the bound (6).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis,\nthe \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nWrite a line in reduced integer form  \n\n\\[\np\\,x+q\\,y=r ,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\; \\gcd(p,q)=1,\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nwith the restrictions  \n\n\\[\np\\neq0,\\qquad p\\neq -q .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n(If \\(p=0\\) the line is horizontal; if \\(q=0\\) it is vertical; if\n\\(p=-q\\) it has slope \\(-1\\).)  \nFrom (2) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nx\\equiv r\\,p^{-1}\\pmod q .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus the \\(x\\)-coordinates of the integer points of the line form a single\nresidue class modulo \\(|q|\\).  Consequently the values of the sum\n\\(a+b\\) on the line differ by the constant \\(|p+q|\\); in particular a\nsunny line meets each diagonal  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta_s:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b=s\\},\\qquad\n2\\le s\\le n+1,\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nin **at most one** point, and because \\(|p+q|\\ge2\\) it can meet at most\n\\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) of the diagonals.  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{A sunny line contains at most }m:=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac n2\\Bigr\\rceil\n      \\text{ points of }S_n.}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nA non‑sunny line (horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)) intersects the\ntriangle \\(\\{a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) in a segment of length at most \\(n\\); therefore it\ncontains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\nAssume that among the \\(n\\) distinct lines exactly \\(k\\) are sunny.\nCounting the points of \\(S_n\\) gives\n\n\\[\nk\\,m+(n-k)n\\ge |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(m=\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) and \\(n-m=\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\), (7) is\nequivalent to  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\\frac{n(n-1)}{2\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nEvaluating (8) yields\n\n\\[\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\nn-2 & (n\\ge4\\text{ even}),\\\\[2mm]\nn-1 & (n\\ge5\\text{ odd}),\\\\[2mm]\nn   & (n=3).\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThus (9) is an upper bound for the number of sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Constructions attaining the bound\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  Even \\(n=2p\\;(p\\ge2)\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le 2p-1,\\;i\\neq p\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=-\\frac{i}{p}\\,x+\\frac{i(p+1)}{p}.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n*Sunny.*  The slope \\(-i/p\\) is never \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore every\n\\(L_i\\) is sunny.\n\n*Points of \\(L_i\\) in \\(S_{2p}\\).*  A straightforward substitution shows that\n\\(L_i\\) contains exactly the \\(p\\) lattice points  \n\n\\[\n(1,i),\\;(2,i-1),\\dots ,\\;(p,i-p+1),\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nall of which satisfy \\(1\\le a,b\\le p\\) and \\(a+b\\le2p\\); hence they belong\nto \\(S_{2p}\\).\n\n*Disjointness.*  For distinct indices \\(i\\neq j\\) the sets (11) are\ndisjoint, because the second coordinate of the first point equals the\nindex itself.\n\nConsequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L:=\\{L_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le 2p-1,\\;i\\neq p\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(2p-2=n-2\\) pairwise disjoint sunny lines, each containing\nexactly \\(p\\) points of \\(S_{2p}\\); together they cover \\((2p-2)p=n-2\\)\npoints of \\(S_{2p}\\).\n\nThe two remaining diagonals are covered by the two non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nD_1:\\;x+y=2p,\\qquad D_2:\\;x+y=2p+1 .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nBoth \\(D_1\\) and \\(D_2\\) contain at most \\(2p\\) points of \\(S_{2p}\\) and\ntogether they cover the whole set \\(S_{2p}\\).  Altogether we have\n\\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(n-2\\) of which are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Odd \\(n=2p+1\\;(p\\ge2)\\)\n\nAgain use the sunny lines \\(L_i\\) defined by (10) for\n\\(i=1,\\dots ,2p-1\\) (now none of them has slope \\(-1\\)).\nEach \\(L_i\\) contains the \\(p+1\\) points listed in (11) and the\nfamily \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{2p-1}\\) covers all points of \\(S_{2p+1}\\) except\n\n\\[\nC_1=(1,p+2),\\qquad C_2=(p+2,1).\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nAdd the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_{p+1}:\\; y=x+(p+1)\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nwhich contains \\(C_1\\), and add the horizontal non‑sunny line  \n\n\\[\nH:\\; y=1 .\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(H\\) contains \\(C_2\\) and all points \\((a,1)\\) with\n\\(1\\le a\\le p+2\\); those points were not covered before.\nNow we have \\((2p-1)+1+1=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(n-1\\) of which\n(the \\(2p-1\\) lines \\(L_i\\) together with \\(L_{p+1}\\)) are sunny, and every\npoint of \\(S_{2p+1}\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\ny=x,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\qquad y=2x-1\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\ncover the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\); therefore \\(k=3\\) is\nattainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Obtaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet a configuration achieving the maximal value \\(k_{\\max}\\) be given\n(the families constructed in §§ 2.1–2.3).  Choose any sunny line \\(L\\) and\nreplace it by a horizontal line \\(y=c\\), where \\(c\\) is the second\ncoordinate of any point of \\(L\\).  The horizontal line contains exactly\nthe points of \\(S_n\\) whose second coordinate equals \\(c\\); those points\nwere already covered by \\(L\\).  Hence the covering property is preserved\nwhile the number of sunny lines decreases by one.  Repeating this\noperation we obtain configurations with any integer \\(k\\) satisfying the\nupper bound (9).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Final answer\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the possible numbers of sunny lines are\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\} &&\\text{if }n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n&\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\} &&\\text{if }n\\ge4\\text{ is even},\\\\[2mm]\n&\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\} &&\\text{if }n\\ge5\\text{ is odd}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\nThe maximal attainable numbers are \\(k_{\\max}=n\\) for \\(n=3\\),\n\\(k_{\\max}=n-2\\) for even \\(n\\ge4\\), and \\(k_{\\max}=n-1\\) for odd\n\\(n\\ge5\\).  All these values are realised by the explicit constructions\ngiven above, and any smaller non‑negative integer \\(k\\) is obtained by\nreplacing sunny lines with horizontal (or vertical) non‑sunny lines. ∎"
