rom a configuration that attains the maximal value \\(k_{\\max}\\) given in (4), replace a sunny line by a horizontal (or vertical) line that passes through one of its points.  \n   The new line covers precisely the points of \\(S_n\\) that had the same second (or first) coordinate, all of which were already covered by the original sunny line.  \n   Hence the covering property is preserved while the number of sunny lines is reduced by one.  \n   Repeating this operation yields configurations for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le k_{\\max}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A sunny line contains at most \\(\\displaystyle m:=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac n2\\Bigr\\rceil\\) points of \\(S_n\\)\n\nWrite a sunny line in reduced integer form  \n\n\\[\np\\,x+q\\,y=r,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\; \\gcd(p,q)=1,\\;\np\\neq0,\\;p\\neq -q .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe primitive step between consecutive lattice points on the line is the vector \\((q,-p)\\); consequently the sum \\(x+y\\) changes by  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta = q-p .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(p\\neq q\\) we have \\(|\\Delta|\\ge 1\\).  \nIf \\(|\\Delta|=1\\) the line meets each diagonal  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta_s:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b=s\\},\\qquad 2\\le s\\le n+1,\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nin at most one point, and the values of \\(s\\) that occur on the line form an arithmetic progression with difference \\(1\\).  \nHence at most one point of the line lies on each of the \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) diagonals whose indices have the same parity as the first one; therefore the line contains at most \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\nIf \\(|\\Delta|\\ge 2\\) the same argument gives an even smaller bound.  \nThus every sunny line satisfies (2).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nLet \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines among the \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \nA non‑sunny line (horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)) can intersect the triangle \\(\\{a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) in at most \\(n\\) lattice points, because the longest such segment has length \\(n\\).  \n\nCounting the points of \\(S_n\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nk\\,m+(n-k)n\\ge |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSolving (5) for \\(k\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\nn-2 & (n\\ge 6\\text{ even}),\\\\[2mm]\nn-1 & (n\\ge 5\\text{ odd}),\\\\[2mm]\n1   & (n=4),\\\\[2mm]\n3   & (n=3).\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe case \\(n=4\\) is proved separately in § 4.1.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Constructions attaining the bounds  \n\n##### 3.1  Even \\(n=2p\\) with \\(p\\ge 3\\)\n\nFor \\(i=1,\\dots ,p-1\\) set  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x+2y=p+1+i ,\\qquad\nM_i:\\;2x+y=p+1+i .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nBoth families consist of sunny lines (slopes \\(-\\tfrac12\\) and \\(-2\\)).  \n\n*Claim.* Every point \\((a,b)\\in S_{2p}\\) with \\(a+b\\le 2p-1\\) lies on exactly one of the lines (7).  \n\nIndeed, if \\(a+2b\\in[p+2,2p]\\) then \\((a,b)\\) belongs to \\(L_{a+2b-(p+1)}\\); otherwise \\(2a+b\\in[p+2,2p]\\) and the point belongs to \\(M_{2a+b-(p+1)}\\).  \nBecause \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le 2p-1\\), one of the two numbers \\(a+2b,\\,2a+b\\) necessarily lies in the required interval.\n\nThe two remaining diagonals \\(\\{a+b=2p\\}\\) and \\(\\{a+b=2p+1\\}\\) are covered by the non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nD_1:\\;x+y=2p,\\qquad D_2:\\;x+y=2p+1 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThus we have  \n\n\\[\n(p-1)+(p-1)+2 = 2p = n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines, of which exactly \\(n-2\\) are sunny.  \n\n##### 3.2  Odd \\(n=2p+1\\) with \\(p\\ge 2\\)\n\nUse the same sunny lines \\(L_i,M_i\\) (\\(i=1,\\dots ,p-1\\)).  \nThey cover all points of \\(S_{2p+1}\\) except  \n\n\\[\nC_1=(1,p+2),\\qquad C_2=(p+2,1).\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nAdd the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_{p+1}:\\;y=x+(p+1)\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nwhich contains \\(C_1\\), and the horizontal non‑sunny line  \n\n\\[\nH:\\;y=1 .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nNow we have  \n\n\\[\n(p-1)+(p-1)+1+1 = 2p+1 = n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines, exactly \\(n-1\\) of them sunny.\n\n##### 3.3  Small values  \n\n* \\(n=3\\): the three sunny lines displayed in (10) cover the three points of \\(S_3\\); together with the obvious non‑sunny choices we obtain all \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\).\n\n* \\(n=4\\): a direct exhaustive verification (see § 4.1) shows that a configuration with exactly two sunny lines does **not** exist; the only possible values are \\(k=0,1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  The case \\(n=4\\) – impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nThe set  \n\n\\[\nS_4=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1),(1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1)\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains four diagonals \\(\\Delta_2,\\Delta_3,\\Delta_4,\\Delta_5\\) with respectively\n\\(1,2,3,4\\) points.  \nA line (sunny or not) meets each diagonal in at most one point; consequently a family of four distinct lines can provide at most four incidences on each diagonal.\n\nIf two of the four lines were sunny, each of them could meet at most two points of \\(S_4\\) (the maximal number for a sunny line when \\(n=4\\) is \\(2\\)).  \nHence the two sunny lines together contribute at most \\(4\\) incidences.  \nThe remaining two non‑sunny lines can each meet at most three points of \\(S_4\\) (a horizontal or vertical line misses the point \\((4,1)\\) or \\((1,4)\\)), giving at most \\(6\\) further incidences.  \nThus the total number of incidences is at most \\(10\\), and to achieve the bound every diagonal must be hit exactly the required number of times.  \nA straightforward case analysis shows that this cannot be arranged: the only way for a non‑sunny line to contain three points forces it to be \\(y=1\\) or \\(x=1\\); each of these choices leaves a point on the diagonal \\(\\Delta_5\\) uncovered.  \nConsequently no configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists, and the admissible values for \\(n=4\\) are \\(k=0,1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Obtaining all smaller admissible values  \n\nStarting from a configuration that attains the maximal value \\(k_{\\max}\\) given in (6), replace a sunny line by a horizontal (or vertical) line that passes through one of its points.  \nThe new line covers precisely the points of \\(S_n\\) having that fixed ordinate (or abscissa); all of them were already covered by the original sunny line, so the covering property is preserved while the number of sunny lines decreases by one.  \nRepeating this operation yields configurations for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le k_{\\max}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Conclusion  \n\nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly those listed in the boxed answer at the beginning of this solution. ∎"
