ts of \\(S_4\\):\n\n     \\[\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     &\\text{non‑sunny: } &&y=1,\\\\\n     &\\text{sunny: } &&x+2y=6\\;( (1,4),(2,2) ),\\\\\n     &&&y=x+1\\;( (1,2),(2,3) ),\\\\\n     &&&x+2y=7\\;( (1,3),(3,2) ).\n     \\end{aligned}\n     \\tag{13}\n     \\]\n\n     Hence \\(k_{\\max}=3\\) for \\(n=4\\).  A configuration with exactly two sunny lines is given in the problem statement (or by taking the two lines of (13) together with the two non‑sunny lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\)).\n\n6. **Obtaining every smaller admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Starting from a configuration that attains the maximal value \\(k_{\\max}\\) given in (6), replace a sunny line by a horizontal (or vertical, or diagonal) line that passes through one of its points.  \n   The new line covers precisely the points of \\(S_n\\) having that fixed ordinate (or abscissa, or sum), all of which were already covered by the original sunny line; therefore the covering property is preserved while the number of sunny lines decreases by one.  \n   Repeating this operation yields configurations for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le k_{\\max}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Points on a sunny line  \n\nWrite a sunny line in reduced integer form  \n\n\\[\np\\,x+q\\,y=r,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\; \\gcd(p,q)=1,\\;\np\\neq0,\\;p\\neq -q .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe primitive step between consecutive lattice points on the line is the vector \\((q,-p)\\); consequently the sum \\(x+y\\) changes by  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta = q-p\\neq0 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe line meets each diagonal  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta_s:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b=