                "content": "**Answer**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{array}{ccl}\nn=3 &:&\\{0,1,2,3\\},\\\\[2mm]\nn=4 &:&\\{0,1,2,3\\},\\\\[2mm]\nn\\ge 5 &:&\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}.\n\\end{array}}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 1.  Notation  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(0\\).\n\nFor a line \\(L\\) write it in reduced integer form  \n\n\\[\np\\,x+q\\,y=r ,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\; \\gcd(p,q)=1 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe line is sunny exactly when  \n\n\\[\np\\neq0,\\qquad p\\neq -q .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.  How many points of \\(S_n\\) can a single line contain?  \n\nFrom (1) the primitive step between two consecutive lattice points of \\(L\\) is the vector \\((q,-p)\\); consequently the sum \\(a+b\\) changes by  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta = q-p\\neq0 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nHence a line meets each diagonal  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta_s:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid aqquad 2\\le s\\le n+1,\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nin **at most point**, and the values of \\(s occur form an arithmetic progression with common difference \\(|\\Delta|\\).\n\n*If the line is non‑sunny* (horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)) the whole diagonal \\(\\Delta_s\\) lies on the line only when \\(s\\) equals the constant of the line; therefore a non‑sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \nThe bound \\(n\\) is attained only by the three lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\qquad y=1,\\qquad x+y=n+1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n*If the line is sunny* then \\(|\\Delta|\\ge 1\\).  \nWhen \\(|\\Delta|\\ge 2\\) the progression (3) can meet at most  \n\n\\[\n1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|\\Delta|}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\le\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac n2\\Bigr\\rceil\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\ndifferent diagonals, so the line contains at most \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\nWhen \\(|\\Delta|=1\\) we have \\(q-p=\\pm1\\); then \\(|p|\\ge2\\) or \\(|q|\\ge2\\).  \nBecause one coordinate changes by at least \\(2\\) while the other changes by \\(1\\), positivity of the coordinates forces the line to leave the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) after at most \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) steps.  \nThus **every sunny line contains at most**\n\n\\[\nm:=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac n2\\Bigr\\rceil\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 3.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nAssume that among the \\(n\\) distinct lines exactly \\(k\\ the points of \\(S_n\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\nk\\,m+(n-k)\\,n\\;\\ge\\;|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n*Even \\(n=2p\\).*  Here \\(m=p\\).  Inequality (8) becomes  \n\n\\[\nk p+(2p-k)2p\\ge p(2p+1)\n\\Longrightarrow p(2p-k)\\ge p\\Longrightarrow k\\le 2p-2=n-2 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n*Odd \\(n=2p+1\\).*  Here \\(m=p+1\\).  Inequality (8) gives  \n\n\\[\nk(p+1)+(2p+1-k)(2p+1)\\ge (2p+1)(p+1)\n\\Longrightarrow (2p+1)(2p+1-k)\\ge (2p+1)(p+1)\n\\Longrightarrow k\\le 2p-1=n-2 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThus for every \\(n\\ge5\\)\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2 } .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=3,4\\) the same computation yields \\(k\\le3\\); the examples below show that the bound is sharp.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 4.  Constructions attaining the bound  \n\nWe now exhibit, for every \\(n\\ge5\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.  \nThe construction is different for even and odd \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.1  Even \\(n=2p\\;(p\\ge3)\\)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_i &: x+2y = p+1+i , && i=1,\\dots ,p-1,\\\\\nM_i &: 2x+y = p+1+i , && i=1,\\dots ,p-1,\\\\\nD_1 &: x+y = 2p ,\\\\\nD_2 &: x+y = 2p+1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nThe lines \\(L_i\\) and \\(M_i\\) are sunny (slopes \\(-\\tfrac12\\) and \\(-2\\)).  \nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in S_{2p}\\) set \\(s=a+b\\).\n\n*If \\(s\\le 2p-1\\)* then either \\(a+2b\\) or \\(2a+b\\) lies in the interval\n\\([p+2,2p]\\); consequently the point belongs to exactly one of the lines\n\\(L_i\\) or \\(M_i\\).\n\n*If \\(s=2p\\)* the point lies on \\(D_1\\);  \n*if \\(s=2p+1\\)* it lies on \\(D_2\\).\n\nHence the \\(2p\\) lines (12) cover all points of \\(S_{2p}\\).  \nExactly \\(2(p-1)=2p-2=n-2\\) of them are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.2  Odd \\(n=2p+1\\;(p\\ge2)\\)\n\nStart from the even construction for \\(2p\\) (the lines (12) with the same\n\\(p\\)).  Replace the diagonal \\(D_2:x+y=2p+1\\) by the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD'_2 :\\; x+y = 2p+2 .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nNow we have \\(2p\\) lines, still \\(2p-2\\) of them sunny, and all points of\n\\(S_{2p}\\) together with the whole diagonal \\(x+y=2p+2\\) are covered.\nFinally add one more sunny line, for instance  \n\n\\[\nS :\\; x+2y = 2p+2 .\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(S\\) meets the triangle \\(a+b\\le 2p+2\\) in at most \\(\\lceil(2p+1)/2\\rceil=p+1\\) points, all of which are already covered; therefore the covering property is preserved.  \n\nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n( p-1)+( p-1)+2+1 = 2p+1 = n ,\n\\]\n\nand the number of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n( p-1)+( p-1)+1 = 2p-1 = n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus for every odd \\(n\\ge5\\) we obtain a configuration with exactly\n\\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.3  Small values  \n\n* \\(n=3\\).  The three sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  y=x,\\qquad y=x+1,\\qquad x+2y=5\n  \\]\n\n  cover the six points of \\(S_3k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) are\n  attainable.\n\n* \\(n=4\\).  The four lines  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  &y=1 &&\\text{(non‑sunny)},\\\\\n  &x+2y=6 &&\\text{sunny},\\\\\n  &y=x+1 &&\\text{sunny},\\\\\n  &x+2y=7 &&\\text{sunny},\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n  cover all ten points of \\(S_4\\); therefore \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) are\n  possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 5.  Obtaining every smaller admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFrom a configuration that attains the maximal value \\(k_{\\max}=n-2\\) (the\nconstructions of Sections 4.1 and 4.2) we can obtain any smaller\nnon‑negative integer \\(k\\) as follows.\n\nPick a sunny line \\(L\\) and a point \\(P\\in L\\cap S_n\\).  \nReplace \\(L\\) by a non‑sunny line that also passes through \\(P\\) (choose\n\\(x=\\)const, \\(y=\\)const or \\(x+y=\\)const according to the coordinates of\n\\(P\\)).  \nAll points that were on \\(L\\) remain covered, because they all lie on the\nnew line as well (the new line contains the whole set of points of the\nchosen diagonal, row or column).  \nThus the covering property is preserved while the number of sunny lines\ndecreases by one.  Repeating the operation yields configurations for every\n\\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le k_{\\max}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 6.  Conclusion  \n\n* For \\(n=3\\) and \\(n=4\\) every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le3\\) can be\n  realised.\n* For every \\(n\\ge5\\) the maximal possible number of sunny lines is\n  \\(n-2\\); consequently the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n  \\[\n  0,1,\\dots ,n-2 .\n  \\]\n\nThese are precisely the sets stated in the boxed answer. ∎"
