-----\n\n### 3.  An elementary upper bound for the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines  \n\nAssume that among the required \\(n\\) distinct lines exactly \\(k\\) are sunny.\nCounting the points of \\(S_n\\) we obtain the necessary inequality  \n\n\\[\nk\\,m+(n-k)\\,n\\;\\ge\\;|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(n\\) is even*, write \\(n=2p\\); then \\(m=p\\) and (6) becomes  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n}{2}k+n(n-k)\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n\\Longrightarrow n-k\\ge1 .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(n\\) is odd*, write \\(n=2p+1\\); then \\(m=p+1=\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) and (6) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n+1}{2}k+n(n-k)\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n\\Longrightarrow k\\le n .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThus (7) and (8) are the only restrictions that follow from a simple\ncounting argument.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 4.  Why the bound \\(k=n\\) is impossible for odd \\(n\\)\n\nAssume that \\(n=2p+1\\) is odd and that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.\nBecause \\(|S_n|=n\\,m\\) with \\(m=p+1\\), equality must hold in (6); consequently\n\n* every sunny line contains **exactly** \\(m\\) points of \\(S_n\\);\n* the sets of points belonging to different lines are pairwise disjoint.\n\nFor a sunny line to contain \\(m\\) points we must have \\(|\\Delta|=1\\) in (3);\notherwise the arithmetic progression of the sums \\(a+b\\) would skip at least\none diagonal and the line could meet at most \\(\\lfloor m/2\\rfloor\\) diagonals.\nHence each line satisfies  \n\n\\[\nq-p=\\pm1 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(q=p+1\\) (the case \\(q=p-1\\) is analogous) and using (1) we obtain\nthe family of lines  \n\n\\[\np\\,x+(p+1)\\,y=r ,\\qquad r\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed \\(r\\) the line (10) meets the diagonals\n\\(\\Delta_{s}\\) with \\(s\\) ranging over a **consecutive** block of\n\\(m\\) integers; on each of those diagonals it contributes exactly one point.\nConsequently the collection of all \\(n\\) lines yields a partition of the set\n\\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) of diagonals into \\(n\\) blocks, each block having\nlength \\(m\\).\n\nLet \\(c_s\\) denote the number of lines that intersect diagonal \\(\\Delta_s\\).\nBecause the blocks are disjoint, \\(c_s\\) equals the number of blocks that\ncontain the integer \\(s\\).  Since each block has length \\(m\\),\n\n\\[\nc_s\\equiv m\\pmod 2\\qquad\\text{for every }s .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, moving from diagonal \\(s\\) to \\(s+1\\) either stays inside the same\nblock (no change of \\(c_s\\)) or leaves a block and enters another one\n(both changes are by \\(1\\)).  Hence the parity of \\(c_s\\) is constant and\nequal to the parity of the block length \\(m\\).\n\nNow the number of points on diagonal \\(\\Delta_s\\) is \\(s-1\\); therefore we must\nhave  \n\n\\[\nc_s = s-1\\qquad (2\\le s\\le n+1).\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nThe right–hand side of (12) alternates in parity:\n\\(s-1\\) is odd for even \\(s\\) and even for odd \\(s\\).\nBut (11) says that all the numbers \\(c_s\\) have the **same** parity\n(the parity of \\(m\\)).  This is impossible because the set\n\\(\\{s-1\\mid 2\\le s\\le n+1\\}\\) contains both even and odd integers.\nHence the assumption that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny leads to a contradiction.\nTherefore for odd \\(n\\)\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1 .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 5.  Upper bounds summarised  \n\n\\[\nk_{\\max}=\n\\begin{cases}\n3, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n3, & n=4,\\\\[2mm]\nn-1, & n\\ge5\\text{ odd},\\\\[2mm]\nn-2, & n\\ge6\\text{ even}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\n(The bound \\(n-2\\) for even \\(n\\) follows from (7) together with the\nobservation that at least two of the three special non‑sunny lines\n(2) are needed; a single non‑sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points,\nso with only one non‑sunny line the remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines could\ncover at most \\((n-1)\\,\\frac n2 < |S_n|\\) when \\(n\\ge6\\).)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 6.  Constructions attaining the bounds  \n\nAll constructions use only integer coefficients, hence the lines are distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.1  Even \\(n=2p\\;(p\\ge3)\\) – exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_i &: x+2y = p+1+i , && i=1,\\dots ,p-1,\\\\\nM_i &: 2x+y = p+1+i , && i=1,\\dots ,p-1,\\\\\nD_1 &: x+y = 2p,\\\\\nD_2 &: x+y = 2p+1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nThe lines \\(L_i\\) and \\(M_i\\) have slopes \\(-\\tfrac12\\) and \\(-2\\); they are\nsunny.  Each point \\((a,b)\\in S_{2p}\\) with \\(a+b\\le 2p-1\\) satisfies\neither \\(a+2b\\in\\{p+2,\\dots ,2p\\}\\) or \\(2a+b\\in\\{p+2,\\dots ,2p\\}\\),\nhence lies on exactly one of the lines (15).  \nThe two remaining diagonals \\(\\{a+b=2p\\}\\) and \\(\\{a+b=2p+1\\}\\) are covered\nby the non‑sunny lines \\(D_1,D_2\\).  \nThus we have \\((p-1)+(p-1)+2=2p=n\\) distinct lines, of which\n\\(n-2\\) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.2  Odd \\(n=2p+1\\;(p\\ge2)\\) – exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines  \n\nUse the same families \\(L_i,M_i\\) as in (15) (now \\(i=1,\\dots ,p-1\\)).\nThey cover all points of \\(S_{2p+1}\\) except  \n\n\\[\nC_1=(1,p+2),\\qquad C_2=(p+2,1).\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nAdd the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nC:\\; y = x + (p+1)\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nwhich contains both \\(C_1\\) and \\(C_2\\), and the non‑sunny horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nH:\\; y = 1 .\n\\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nThe total number of lines is \\((p-1)+(p-1)+1+1 = 2p+1 = n\\);\nexactly \\(n-1\\) of them are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.3  Small values  \n\n* \\(n=3\\): the three sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  y=x,\\qquad y=x+1,\\qquad x+2y=5\n  \\tag{19}\n  \\]\n\n  cover the six points of \\(S_3\\); hence every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) is\n  attainable (the cases \\(k<3\\) are obtained by replacing sunny lines with\n  the three non‑sunny lines (2)).\n\n* \\(n=4\\): the four lines  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  &y=1 &&\\text{(non‑sunny)},\\\\\n  &x+2y=6 &&\\text{sunny},\\\\\n  &y=x+1 &&\\text{sunny},\\\\\n  &x+2y=7 &&\\text{sunny},\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\tag{20}\n  \\]\n\n  cover all ten points of \\(S_4\\); thus \\(k_{\\max}=3\\).  Replacing sunny\n  lines by the three non‑sunny lines (2) yields configurations for every\n  \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 7.  Obtaining every smaller admissible \\(k\\)\n\nStarting from a configuration that attains the maximal value\n\\(k_{\\max}\\) given in (14), pick a sunny line \\(L\\) and a point\n\\(P\\in L\\cap S_n\\).  Replace \\(L\\) by a non‑sunny line that passes through\n\\(P\\) (choose \\(x=\\) constant, \\(y=\\) constant or \\(x+y=\\) constant\naccording to the coordinates of \\(P\\)).  The new line covers exactly the\npoints of \\(S_n\\) having that fixed ordinate, abscissa or sum; all those\npoints were already covered by \\(L\\).  Hence the covering property is\npreserved while the number of sunny lines decreases by one.  Repeating the\noperation produces configurations for every integer  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le k_{\\max}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 8.  Conclusion  \n\nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines for the given problem are exactly the\nsets displayed in the boxed answer at the beginning of this solution.  ∎\n"
