 \\(-2\\)).  \n   Every point \\((a,b)\\in S_{2p}\\) with \\(a+b\\le 2p-1\\) lies on exactly one of the lines (9).  \n   The two remaining diagonals \\(\\{a+b=2p\\}\\) and \\(\\{a+b=2p+1\\}\\) are covered by the non‑sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   D_1:\\;x+y=2p,\\qquad D_2:\\;x+y=2p+1 .\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus we have \\((p-1)+(p-1)+2=2p=n\\) distinct lines, of which exactly \\(n-2\\) are sunny.\n\n   *Odd \\(n=2p+1\\).*  \n   Use the same sunny lines \\(L_i,M_i\\;(i=1,\\dots ,p-1)\\) as above (they cover all points with\n   \\(a+b\\le 2p\\)).  \n   The three top diagonals \\(\\{a+b=2p+1\\},\\{a+b=2p+2\\},\\{a+b=2p+3\\}\\) are covered by the three non‑sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   D_1:\\;x+y=2p+1,\\qquad \n   D_2:\\;x+y=2p+2,\\qquad \n   D_3:\\;x+y=2p+3 .\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   The total number of lines is \\((p-1)+(p-1)+3=2p+1=n\\); exactly \\(n-2\\) of them are sunny.\n\n   The constructions above work for every \\(n\\ge5\\) and give a configuration with\n   \\(k=n-2\\).\n\n7. **Obtaining every smaller admissible \\(k\\).**  \n\n   Starting from a configuration with the maximal possible number \\(k_{\\max}=n-2\\) of sunny lines, pick a sunny line \\(L\\) and a point \\(P\\in L\\cap S_n\\).  \n   Replace \\(L\\) by a non‑sunny line that passes through \\(P\\) (choose \\(x=1\\), \\(y=1\\) or \\(x+y=c\\) according to the coordinates of \\(P\\)).  \n   All points that were covered by \\(L\\) remain covered, the covering property is preserved, and the number of sunny lines decreases by one.  \n   Repeating this operation yields configurations for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n8. **Small values \\(n=3,4\\).**  \n\n   Direct constructions (for instance the three sunny lines\n   \\(y=x,\\;y=x+1,\\;x+2y=5\\) for \\(n=3\\) and the four lines displayed in (13) of the\n   previous solution for \\(n=4\\)) show that all numbers \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) are attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line\n\\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Points on a sunny line  \n\nWrite a sunny line in reduced integer form  \n\n\\[\np\\,x+q\\,y=r,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\; \\gcd(p,q)=1,\\;\np\\neq0,\\;p\\neq -q .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe primitive step between consecutive lattice points on the line is the vector\n\\((q,-p)\\); consequently the sum \\(x+y\\) changes by  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta = q-p\\neq0 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nHence the line meets each diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_s:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b=s\\},\\qquad 2\\le s\\le n+1,\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nin at most one point, and the values of \\(s\\) that occur form an arithmetic\nprogression with common difference \\(|\\Delta|\\).  \nTherefore a sunny line can intersect at most  \n\n\\[\nm:=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac n2\\Bigr\\rceil\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\ndifferent diagonals, i.e. it contains at most \\(m\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Points on a non‑sunny line  \n\nA non‑sunny line is horizontal (\\(y=c\\)), vertical (\\(x=c\\)) or has slope \\(-1\\)\n(\\(x+y=c\\)).  \nInside the triangle \\(\\{a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) such a line contains at most \\(n\\) lattice\npoints; the bound is attained only by the three lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\qquad y=1,\\qquad x+y=n+1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  At least two non‑sunny lines are necessary for \\(n\\ge5\\)\n\nEvery sunny line meets the vertical line \\(x=1\\) in at most one lattice point,\nand likewise meets the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) in at most one lattice point.\nConsequently a sunny line contains **at most one** point of the set  \n\n\\[\nA:=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\cup\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nwhich consists of \\(2n-1\\) distinct points of \\(S_n\\).\n\nA non‑sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(A\\) (the three lines in\n(6) are the only ones that achieve this bound).  \nIf we used at most one non‑sunny line, at least \\((2n-1)-n=n-1\\) points of \\(A\\)\nwould have to be covered by sunny lines, each of which can cover **only one**\nsuch point.  Hence we would need at least \\(n-1\\) distinct sunny lines merely for\nthe points of \\(A\\).  Together with the remaining points of \\(S_n\\) this would\nrequire more than the available \\(n\\) lines, a contradiction.\n\nThus for every \\(n\\ge5\\) any admissible family of \\(n\\) lines must contain **at\nleast two non‑sunny lines**.  Consequently\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2\\qquad (n\\ge5).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  General upper bound\n\nLet \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines among the \\(n\\) distinct lines.\nCounting the points of \\(S_n\\) and using the bounds from Sections 1 and 2 we have  \n\n\\[\nk\\,m+(n-k)\\,n\\;\\ge\\;|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nSolving (9) gives the same inequality (8) for \\(n\\ge5\\); for the small cases\n\\(n=3,4\\) it yields \\(k\\le3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Constructions attaining the bound \\(k=n-2\\)\n\nWrite \\(n=2p\\) (even) or \\(n=2p+1\\) (odd) with \\(p\\ge2\\).\n\n*Even \\(n=2p\\).*  \nFor \\(i=1,\\dots ,p-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x+2y=p+1+i ,\\qquad \nM_i:\\;2x+y=p+1+i .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nBoth families consist of sunny lines (slopes \\(-\\tfrac12\\) and \\(-2\\)).  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_{2p}\\) with \\(a+b\\le 2p-1\\) either \\(a+2b\\) or \\(2a+b\\) lies\nin the interval \\([p+2,2p]\\); consequently the point belongs to exactly one of the\nlines (10).  \n\nThe two remaining diagonals \\(\\{a+b=2p\\}\\) and \\(\\{a+b=2p+1\\}\\) are covered by the\nnon‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nD_1:\\;x+y=2p,\\qquad D_2:\\;x+y=2p+1 .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThus we have \\((p-1)+(p-1)+2=2p=n\\) distinct lines, of which exactly \\(n-2\\) are\nsunny.\n\n*Odd \\(n=2p+1\\).*  \nAgain use the sunny lines \\(L_i,M_i\\;(i=1,\\dots ,p-1)\\); they cover all points of\n\\(S_{2p+1}\\) with \\(a+b\\le 2p\\).  \nThe three top diagonals \\(\\{a+b=2p+1\\},\\{a+b=2p+2\\},\\{a+b=2p+3\\}\\) are covered by  \n\n\\[\nD_1:\\;x+y=2p+1,\\qquad \nD_2:\\;x+y=2p+2,\\qquad \nD_3:\\;x+y=2p+3 .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nNow \\((p-1)+(p-1)+3=2p+1=n\\) distinct lines are obtained, and exactly \\(n-2\\) of\nthem are sunny.\n\nHence for every \\(n\\ge5\\) a configuration with \\(k=n-2\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Obtaining every smaller admissible \\(k\\)\n\nStart from a configuration with the maximal number \\(k_{\\max}=n-2\\) of sunny\nlines.  Choose a sunny line \\(L\\) and a point \\(P\\in L\\cap S_n\\).  Replace \\(L\\)\nby a non‑sunny line that passes through \\(P\\) (take \\(x=1\\), \\(y=1\\) or\n\\(x+y=c\\) according to the coordinates of \\(P\\)).  All points that were covered\nby \\(L\\) remain covered, the covering property is preserved, and the number of\nsunny lines decreases by one.  Repeating this operation yields configurations for\nevery integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Small values \\(n=3,4\\)\n\n* \\(n=3\\).  The three sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  y=x,\\qquad y=x+1,\\qquad x+2y=5\n  \\]\n\n  cover the six points of \\(S_3\\); thus all \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) are attainable.\n\n* \\(n=4\\).  The four lines  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  &y=1 &&\\text{(non‑sunny)},\\\\\n  &x+2y=6 &&\\text{sunny},\\\\\n  &y=x+1 &&\\text{sunny},\\\\\n  &x+2y=7 &&\\text{sunny},\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n  cover all ten points of \\(S_4\\); consequently every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) can be\n  realised (for \\(k=0,1,2