he argument of step 5 with \\(k=3\\) shows this).  \n\n   Hence the answer stated in the verdict is complete.\n\n--- \n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Constructions giving \\(k=0,1,3\\)\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  The \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,x=2,\\dots ,x=n\\) are non‑sunny and together contain every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).  \n  The only uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\); it lies on any sunny line, e.g. the line \\(y=x\\).  Thus we obtain a configuration with exactly one sunny line.\n\n* **\\(k=3\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\).**  \n  Take the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\).  \n  The remaining points are those with \\(a\\ge n-2\\); after the translation \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a-(n-3),b)\\) they form the triangle \\(T_3\\).  \n  The three sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  L_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n  L_2:\\;y-2=-\\tfrac12(x-1),\\qquad\n  L_3:\\;y-3=-2(x-1)\n  \\]\n\n  cover \\(T_3\\) (each contains two points of the left side and one point of the bottom side).  Adding the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines gives a configuration with exactly three sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Even numbers of sunny lines are impossible\n\nLet a covering of \\(T_k\\) by \\(k\\) sunny lines be given.  \nA sunny line is not parallel to any side of the triangle, hence it meets the boundary in at most two points; consequently it contains at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_k\\).  Therefore the total number of distinct points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(k\\) is even, \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=