or \\(i=2,\\dots ,k\\)\n\n\\[\n\\ell_i\\; \\text{contains}\\;(1,i)\\ \\text{and}\\ (\\sigma(i),1),\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nand the remaining line \\(\\ell_1\\) contains \\((1,1)\\).\n\nBecause the slopes of the lines \\(\\ell_i\\;(i\\ge2)\\) are \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{1-i}{\\sigma(i)-1}\\), none of them is horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\); therefore they are indeed sunny.\n\n#### 3.2  The point \\((2,k-1)\\) cannot be covered\n\nConsider the interior point  \n\n\\[\nP=(2,k-1)\\in T_k\\qquad(k\\ge5).\n\\]\n\nSuppose that \\(P\\) lies on some line \\(\\ell_i\\) with \\(i\\ge2\\).  \nUsing (2) and the collinearity of the three points \\((1,i),(\\sigma(i),1),P\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\n(k-1-i)\\bigl(\\sigma(i)-1\\bigr)=1-i .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(i<k\\) then the left‑hand side of (3) is non‑negative whereas the right‑hand side is negative, a contradiction.  \nIf \\(i=k\\) then (3) forces \\(\\sigma(k)=k\\); the line through \\((1,k)\\) and \\((k,1)\\) has slope \\(-1\\) and is therefore **not sunny**.  \nHence \\(P\\) cannot belong to any \\(\\ell_i\\) with \\(i\\ge2\\).\n\nThe only possible line containing \\(P\\) is \\(\\ell_1\\).  The line \\(\\ell_1\\) already uses one of its \\(\\frac{k+1}{2}\\) points for \\((1,1)\\); consequently it can contain at most \\(\\frac{k-1}{2}\\) interior points.  The remaining \\(k-1\\) lines each already contain two boundary points, so each of them can contain at most \\(\\frac{k+1}{2}-2=\\frac{k-3}{2}\\) interior points.  Therefore the total number of interior points that can be covered is at most\n\n\\[\n\\frac{k-1}{2}+(k-1)\\frac{k-3}{2}\n   =\\frac{k^{2}-3k+2}{2}\n   =\\frac{(k-1)(k-2)}{2},\n\\]\n\nwhich equals the actual number of interior points of \\(T_k\\).  Equality would require \\(\\ell_1\\) to contain exactly \\(\\frac{k-1}{2}\\) interior points, i.e. to use **all** its remaining slots for interior points.  But \\(\\ell_1\\) already contains the point \\((1,1)\\); the only way to reach the bound is for \\(\\ell_1\\) to co