ines.**  \n   Let \\(s\\) be the number of sunny lines.  \n   By Lemma 1 they cover at most \\(2s\\) boundary points, so the remaining \\(B_n-2s\\) boundary points must be covered by the \\(n-s\\) non‑sunny lines.  \n   A non‑sunny line (vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\)) can contain at most \\(n\\) boundary points (Lemma 2). Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-s)n\\ge B_n-2s\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad s\\le\\frac{n^2-3n+3}{\\,n-2\\,}=n-1+\\frac1{n-2}.\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   In particular \\(s\\le n\\).  This bound does not forbid \\(s=3\\) for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n5. **Lemma 3 (exactly two sunny lines are impossible).**  \n   For every \\(n\\ge3\\) a covering of \\(T_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines cannot contain exactly two sunny lines.  \n\n   *Proof.*  \n   The side \\(S_1\\) consists of the points \\((i,1)\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\).  \n   Each sunny line meets \\(S_1\\) in at most one point, so the two sunny lines together cover at most two points of \\(S_1\\).  \n   Consequently the \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines must cover the remaining \\(n-2\\) points of \\(S_1\\).  \n\n   A non‑sunny line is either vertical (\\(x=c\\)), horizontal (\\(y=c\\)) or of slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)).  \n   Only a vertical line can contain more than one point of \\(S_1\\); a horizontal or slope \\(-1\\) line meets \\(S_1\\) in exactly one point.  \n   Hence **all** the \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines must be distinct vertical lines, each covering a different point of \\(S_1\\).  \n\n   Let the two vertical columns that are *not* used be \\(x=i\\) and \\(x=j\\) (\\(i\\neq j\\)).  \n   The points \\((i,1)\\) and \\((j,1)\\) on the horizontal side \\(S_2\\) are not covered by any vertical line, so they must be covered by the two sunny lines.  \n   A sunny line can meet \\(S_2\\) in at most one point (Lemma 1), therefore each sunny line contains exactly one of \\((i,1)\\) and \\((j,1)\\).  \n\n   The same columns contain the points \\((i,n+1-i)\\) and \\((j,n+1-j)\\) on the hypotenuse \\(S_3\\).  \n   No vertical line covers them, and a sunny line that already contains a point of \\(S_2\\) cannot also contain a point of \\(S_3\\) (otherwise the three points would be collinear, contradicting the fact that the line is not parallel to any side).  \n   Hence the two sunny lines cannot cover the two points of \\(S_3\\).  \n   At least one boundary point would remain uncovered – a contradiction. ∎  \n\n   The argument above does **not** use any property that fails for \\(n=3\\); therefore Lemma 3 holds for all \\(n\\ge3\\).  Consequently \\(k=2\\) is impossible for every admissible \\(n\\).\n\n6. **Existence of the remaining values of \\(k\\).**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n   \\]\n\n   All are non‑sunny and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on one of them.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\); they cover all points of \\(T_n\\) except \\((n,1)\\).  \n   Add the sunny line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\ell:\\;y-1=\\frac{1}{1-n}(x-n),\n   \\]\n\n   whose slope \\(\\frac{1}{1-n}\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   Exactly one line is sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*  \n\n   - **Case \\(n=3\\).**  \n     The three sunny lines  \n\n     \\[\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     \\ell_1 &: y = x,\\\\\n     \\ell_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\\\\n     \\ell_3 &: y = -2x+5,\n     \\end{aligned}\n     \\]\n\n     have slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\) (none is \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \n     They pass respectively through the pairs \\((1,1),(2,2)\\); \\((1,2),(3,1)\\); \\((1,3),(2,1)\\).  \n     Hence every point of \\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\) lies on at least one of the three lines, and the three lines are distinct and sunny.\n\n   - **Case \\(n=4\\).**  \n     Take the two non‑sunny side‑lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\).  \n     The six remaining points are  \n\n     \\[\n     (2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(3,1),(3,2),(4,1),\n     \\]\n\n     which are covered by  \n\n     \\[\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     \\ell_1 &: y = x-1,\\\\\n     \\ell_2 &: y = -2x+5,\\\\\n     \\ell_3 &: y = -\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32 .\n     \\end{aligned}\n     \\]\n\n   - **Case \\(n=5\\).**  \n     Again use \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\); the six uncovered points  \n\n     \\[\n     (2,2),(2,3),(2,4),(3,2),(3,3),(4,2)\n     \\]\n\n     are covered by  \n\n     \\[\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     \\ell_1 &: y = x,\\\\\n     \\ell_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\\\\n     \\ell_3 &: y = -2x+8 .\n     \\end{aligned}\n     \\]\n\n   - **Case \\(n\\ge6\\).**  \n     First place the three non‑sunny side‑lines  \n\n     \\[\n     x=1,\\qquad y=1,\\qquad x+y=n+1 .\n     \\]\n\n     They cover all points on the three sides of the triangle.  \n     The points that remain uncovered form a translated copy of the triangle \\(T_3\\):\n\n     \\[\n     \\{(a+n-3,b)\\mid a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le4\\}.\n     \\]\n\n     The three sunny lines  \n\n     \\[\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     \\ell_1 &: y = x-(n-3),\\\\\n     \\ell_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12\\bigl(x-(n-3)\\bigr)+\\tfrac52,\\\\\n     \\ell_3 &: y = -2\\bigl(x-(n-3)\\bigr)+5 ,\n     \\end{aligned}\n     \\]\n\n     have slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\) and cover exactly this translated copy.  \n     Thus we have three sunny lines and three non‑sunny side‑lines.  \n     If \\(n>6\\) we may add the remaining \\(n-6\\) vertical lines \\(x=2,3,\\dots ,n-4\\); they are non‑sunny and do not affect the already covered points.  \n     Hence for every \\(n\\ge4\\) we obtain a configuration with exactly three sunny lines.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 3 shows that \\(k=2\\) is impossible for any \\(n\\ge3\\).  \n   The explicit constructions give realizations of \\(k=0,1\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\) and of \\(k=3\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\) (the case \\(n=3\\) is handled separately).  \n   Therefore the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,3\\}}\\qquad\\text{for all }n\\ge3 .\n   \