he $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the numbers of sunny lines that can occur in a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,3\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Geometric setting.**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   the right‑angled lattice triangle whose sides are  \n\n   \\[\n   S_1:\\;x=1,\\qquad S_2:\\;y=1,\\qquad S_3:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   The numbers of points are  \n\n   \\[\n   B_n=|\\,\\partial T_n\\,|=3n-3,\\qquad \n   N_n=|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 ,\\qquad \n   I_n=N_n-B_n=\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Lines and “sunny’’ property.**  \n   A line is *non‑sunny* iff it is parallel to one of the three sides; otherwise it is *sunny*.  \n   Hence a non‑sunny line is either vertical \\((x=c)\\), horizontal \\((y=c)\\) or of slope \\(-1\\) \\((x+y=c)\\).\n\n3. **Boundary points on a line.**  \n   *Lemma 1.*  \n   – A sunny line meets each side of the triangle in at most one point, therefore it contains at most **two** boundary points.  \n   – A non‑sunny line that coincides with a side contains all \\(n\\) points of that side; any other non‑sunny line contains at most two boundary points.\n\n4. **How many side‑parallel lines are forced?**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   v=\\begin{cases}1&\\text{if the side }x=1\\text{ is used,}\\\\0&\\text{otherwise,}\\end{cases}\\qquad\n   h=\\begin{cases}1&\\text{if the side }y=1\\text{ is used,}\\\\0&\\text{otherwise,}\\end{cases}\\qquad\n   d=\\begin{cases}1&\\text{if the side }x+y=n+1\\text{ is used,}\\\\0&\\text{otherwise.}\\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n   Denote by \\(V,H,D\\) the numbers of vertical, horizontal and diagonal (\\(x+y=\\text{const}\\)) lines, and by \\(S\\) the number of sunny lines.  \n   The covering condition on the three sides gives\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &\\text{(side }x=1):\\qquad n-vn\\le\\text{# of non‑vertical lines}=n-V,\\\\\n   &\\text{(side }y=1):\\qquad n-hn\\le n-H,\\\\\n   &\\text{(side }x+y=n+1):\\qquad n-dn\\le n-D .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   V\\le vn,\\qquad H\\le hn,\\qquad D\\le dn. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   If a side is **not** used (\\(v=0\\), say), then (1) forces \\(V=0\\); i.e. no line parallel to that side may appear.\n\n5. **At most three sunny lines.**  \n\n   *Lemma 2.* In any admissible configuration we have \\(S\\le 3\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   From (1) we see that to have any sunny line we must have \\(v=h=d=1\\); otherwise one of the families \\(V,H,D\\) would be forced to be empty and the three side‑parallel lines would already occupy all \\(n\\) lines, leaving no sunny line.  \n   Thus the three side lines \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=n+1\\) are present.  \n   Removing them leaves the set  \n\n   \\[\n   T_{n-3}'=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   which is a translate of the triangle \\(T_{n-3}\\) and has  \n\n   \\[\n   B_{n-3}=3(n-3)-3\\quad\\text{boundary points.}\n   \\]\n\n   The remaining \\(n-3\\) lines consist of \\(S\\) sunny lines and at most three non‑sunny lines that are parallel to the three sides (one of each direction).  \n   By Lemma 1 each sunny line contributes at most two boundary points of \\(T_{n-3}'\\); each side‑parallel line contributes at most \\(n-3\\) points of the corresponding side, and any other non‑sunny line contributes at most two.  \n   Consequently the total number of boundary points that can be covered is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   2S+3\\,(n-3)+2\\bigl((n-3)-S-3\\bigr)=2(n-3)+S\\,(n-5).\n   \\]\n\n   Since we must cover all \\(B_{n-3}=3n-12\\) points, we need  \n\n   \\[\n   2(n-3)+S\\,(n-5)\\ge 3n-12\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;S\\le 3 .\n   \\]\n\n   ∎\n\n6. **Impossibility of exactly two sunny lines.**  \n\n   *Lemma 3.* No configuration contains exactly two sunny lines.\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Assume \\(S=2\\). By Lemma 2 we must have the three side lines, so the remaining \\(n-5\\) lines are non‑sunny and not parallel to any side.  \n   Each of those lines meets the side \\(x=1\\) in at most one point, hence together they can cover at most \\(n-5\\) points of that side.  \n   The two sunny lines together cover at most two further points of the side (Lemma 1).  \n   Thus at most \\((n-5)+2=n-3\\) points of the side \\(x=1\\) are covered, but the side contains \\(n\\) points – a contradiction. ∎\n\n7. **Existence of the admissible values.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n   \\]\n\n   All are non‑sunny and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on one of them.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\); they miss only the point \\((n,1)\\).  \n   Add the sunny line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\ell:\\;y-1=\\frac{1}{1-n}\\,(x-n),\n   \\]\n\n   whose slope \\(\\frac{1}{1-n}\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   Exactly one line is sunny and all points are covered.\n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*\n\n   – **Case \\(n=3\\).**  \n     The three sunny lines  \n\n     \\[\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     \\ell_1 &: y = x,\\\\\n     \\ell_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\\\\n     \\ell_3 &: y = -2x+5,\n     \\end{aligned}\n     \\]\n\n     have slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\) and pass respectively through the pairs  \n     \\((1,1),(2,2)\\), \\((1,2),(3,1)\\) and \\((1,3),(2,1)\\).  \n     They cover all six points of \\(T_3\\).\n\n   – **Case \\(n\\ge 4\\).**  \n     Use the three side lines  \n\n     \\[\n     x=1,\\qq