covered.  \n   Each sunny line can intersect any such row in at most one point, so the \\(k\\) sunny lines together can cover at most \\(k\\cdot k=k^{2}\\) points among the uncovered rows.\n\n   *Counting points.*  \n   All points of \\(S_n\\) must be covered, i.e.  \n   \\[\n   \\frac{h(h+1)}2+k^{2}\\;\\ge\\;\\sum_{b=1}^{n}(n+1-b)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(h=n-k\\) into (1) gives  \n   \\[\n   \\frac{(n-k)(n-k+1)}2+k^{2}\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n   Multiplying by \\(2\\) and simplifying yields  \n   \\[\n   k\\bigl(3k-2n-1\\bigr)\\ge0 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(n\\ge3\\) the factor \\(3k-2n-1\\) is negative whenever \\(k\\ge2\\); consequently (2) can hold only for \\(k=0\\) or \\(k=1\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The constructions exhibit configurations with \\(k=0\\) and with \\(k=1\\); inequality (2) proves that no configuration can have \\(k\\ge2\\). Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution ###  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) define the **row**\n\n\\[\nR_b=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\}\n      =\\{(1,b),(2,b),\\dots ,(n+1-b,b)\\},\n\\qquad |R_b|=n+1-b .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\); equivalently it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Elementary observation  \n\n*Lemma 1.*  \nIf a line is not horizontal, then it meets each row \\(R_b\\) in at most one point.\n\n*Proof.*  \nTwo distinct points of the same row have the same \\(y\\)-coordinate. A non‑horizontal line cannot contain two points with the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, otherwise its slope would be \\(0\\). ∎  \n\nThe same argument shows that a vertical line or a line of slope \\(-1\\) also meets any given row in at most one point.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Constructions attaining \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n*\\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_b\\).  \nAll lines are non‑sunny, hence \\(k=0\\) is realizable.\n\n*\\(k=1\\).*  \nTake the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-1\n\\]\n\nand add the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;y=2x+n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThe point \\((1,n)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(n=2\\cdot1+n-2\\), so it lies on \\(L\\).  \nEvery other point of \\(S_n\\) has second coordinate \\(b\\le n-1\\) and therefore lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).  \nExactly one line (namely \\(L\\)) is sunny, so \\(k=1\\) is realizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Upper bound \\(k\\le 1\\)\n\nAssume a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of \\(S_n\\).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nh=\\text{number of horizontal lines},\\qquad\nk=n-h=\\text{number of sunny lines}.\n\\]\n\nDenote by \\(H\\subset\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) the set of row indices that are completely covered by the horizontal lines.  \nA horizontal line \\(y=i\\) covers precisely the row \\(R_i\\).\n\n**(a) Points covered by horizontals.**  \nThe total number of points in the rows belonging to \\(H\\) equals  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i\\in H}(n+1-i).\n\\]\n\nThis sum is minimized when the horizontal rows are the highest‑indexed ones, i.e. when  \n\n\\[\nH=\\{n-h+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nIn that case the row sizes are \\(1,2,\\dots ,h\\) and  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i\\in H}(n+1-i)\\ge\\frac{h(h+1)}2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n**(b) Points that must be covered by sunny lines.**  \nThe rows not in \\(H\\) are exactly \\(n-h=k\\) rows.  \nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line can intersect any such row in at most one point; consequently the \\(k\\) sunny lines together can cover at most \\(k\\cdot k=k^{2}\\) points among those rows.\n\n**(c) Counting all points.**  \nAll points of \\(S_n\\) must be covered, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\frac{h(h+1)}2+k^{2}\\;\\ge\\;\\sum_{b=1}^{n}(n+1-b)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(h=n-k\\) into (4) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-k)(n-k+1)}2+k^{2}\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nMultiplying by \\(2\\) and simplifying yields  \n\n\\[\nk\\bigl(3k-2n-1\\bigr)\\ge0 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge3\\) the factor \\(3k-2n-1\\) is negative whenever \\(k\\ge2\\); therefore (5) can hold only for  \n\n\\[\nk=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad k=1 .\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration contains at most one sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Final answer  \n\nThe constructions of §2 show that \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are indeed attainable.  \nThe inequality proved in §3 shows that no configuration can have \\(k\\ge2\\).  \nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
