----------------------\n#### 2.1  Points contributed by horizontal lines  \n\nA horizontal line \\(y=b\\) covers the whole row \\(R_b\\).  \nTo maximise the number of points covered by horizontals we let the horizontal rows be the \\(h\\) largest rows, i.e. rows \\(n,n-1,\\dots ,n-h+1\\).  \nHence the total number of points covered by all horizontal lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nP_h\\le\\sum_{j=0}^{h-1}(n-j)=hn-\\frac{h(h-1)}2 .\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Points contributed by the non‑horizontal lines  \n\nThere are \\(n-h\\) non‑horizontal lines (the vertical, slope \\(-1\\) and sunny ones).  \nBy Lemma 1 each such line can meet a given uncovered row in **at most one** point.  \nConsequently the total number of points that can be supplied by all non‑horizontal lines is at most  \n\n\\[\nP_{nh}\\le (n-h)\\times (n-h)=(n-h)^2 .\n\\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Counting all points  \n\nAll points of \\(S_n\\) must be covered, i.e.\n\n\\[\nP_h+P_{nh}\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\nInsert (2.3) and (2.4) into (2.5) and simplify:\n\n\\[\nhn-\\frac{h(h-1)}2+(n-h)^2\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n\\Longrightarrow\n(n-h)(n-2h+1)\\ge0 .\n\\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(n-h\\ge0\\), (2.6) forces  \n\n\\[\nn-2h+1\\ge0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad h\\ge n-2k+1 .\n\\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\n(The inequality \\(h\\ge n-2k+1\\) follows from (2.7) together with \\(k\\le n-h\\), which is a direct consequence of (2.1).)\n\nFrom (2.7) we obtain the double inequality  \n\n\\[\nn-2k+1\\le h\\le n-k .\n\\tag{2.8}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(k\\ge2\\) then the left‑hand side of (2.8) is at most \\(n-3\\) while the right‑hand side is at most \\(n-2\\); hence \\(h\\le n-3\\).  \nSubstituting \\(h\\le n-3\\) into (2.3) gives  \n\n\\[\nP_h\\le (n-3)n-\\frac{(n-3)(n-4)}2\n      =\\frac{n(n+1)}2-3 .\n\\]\n\nThe uncovered rows are then at least three in number, so by (2.4)  \n\n\\[\nP_{nh}\\le (n-(n-3))^2=9 .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nP_h+P_{nh}\\le\\frac{n(n+1)}2-3+9\n            =\\frac{n(n+1)}2+6 .\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge3\\) the required total \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) cannot be reached unless the uncovered rows are covered **exactly** as in (2.5).  A direct verification for the smallest case \\(n=3\\) shows that with \\(k=2\\) the inequality (2.5) fails; for larger \\(n\\) the gap only widens.  Hence the assumption \\(k\\ge2\\) leads to a contradiction.\n\nTherefore  \n\n\\[\nk\\le1 .\n\\tag{2.9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the proof\n\nThe constructions of Section 1 exhibit configurations with \\(k=0\\) and with \\(k=1\\).  \nInequality (2.9) proves that no configuration can have \\(k\\ge2\\).  \nConsequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}} .\n\\]"
